<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Common reasons Michigan homeowners end up in appraisal</h2>
<p>Most homeowner disputes that reach appraisal aren’t about whether something happened. The insurer agrees there was a storm, a burst pipe or a kitchen fire. The disagreement is about how much it costs to put the house back. In Michigan, a few patterns come up again and again:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Replacing a few rows of discontinued siding or shingles on a 1960s colonial can leave a visibly mismatched home. Whether the policy pays for a uniform appearance depends on its wording and the facts.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Local building departments may require ice-and-water shield, updated electrical work or egress windows when repairs are permitted. Ordinance or law coverage, if you have it, often carries its own limit.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Actual cash value payments subtract depreciation, sometimes on labor as well as materials, and the held-back amount is usually recoverable only if you repair within the policy’s terms.</li>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> Drywall that was “dried in place,” basement carpet that was only cleaned, or a roof patched instead of replaced.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Michigan houses aren’t one-size-fits-all</h2>
<p>A brick bungalow in Detroit, a Heritage Hill Victorian in Grand Rapids, a split-level in Sterling Heights and a cabin outside Marquette all repair differently. Plaster walls, old-growth trim, steep multi-gable roofs and finished basements each change labor hours and materials. Estimates built on generic assumptions often miss these details. Our job as your appraiser is to describe the actual house, not an average one.</p>
<h3>Older homes and finished basements</h3>
<p>Basements are nearly universal in the Lower Peninsula, and many are finished living space. When water or smoke reaches them, the scope can include framing, insulation, flooring, built-ins and mechanicals. Older homes may also hide lath and plaster, outdated wiring or lead paint that affects how work must be done.</p>
<h2>How a homeowner appraisal unfolds</h2>
<p>Once either side demands appraisal under the policy and Michigan’s statutory framework, each side names its own appraiser. We start by reading your policy and every estimate the insurer has issued, then inspect the home ourselves. From there we prepare a detailed estimate and meet with the insurer’s appraiser. Many items get resolved at that stage. Anything left goes to the umpire, and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount of loss. The <a href="/michigan-appraisal-process/">Michigan appraisal process</a> page lays out each step.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep protecting the property</b>Your policy likely requires you to prevent further damage. Tarping a roof, extracting water or boarding a window shouldn’t wait for appraisal. Keep receipts and photos, because those costs are often part of the claim.</div>
<h2>What to have ready</h2>
<p>Pull together your declarations page and full policy, each version of the insurer’s estimate, the adjuster’s photos if you have them, your own photos, contractor bids and receipts for emergency work. If you’re unsure how far apart the numbers really are, our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> helps frame whether appraisal makes economic sense, and the <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV and ACV holdback calculator</a> shows how depreciation affects the first check.</p>
<h2>What we don’t do</h2>
<p>We’re not a law firm and don’t give legal advice. We don’t act as public adjusters or argue coverage. If your insurer has denied the claim, or you have questions about deadlines or unfair claim practices, a Michigan attorney is the right person to ask. Where the dispute is about dollars and scope, an independent appraiser who knows Michigan homes can make a real difference in how clearly your side is presented.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>Do I need a contractor’s bid before appraisal?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>It helps but isn’t required. Your appraiser prepares an independent estimate, and contractor bids can serve as useful support for pricing and scope.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Will appraisal delay my repairs?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Emergency repairs to prevent further damage should go ahead right away. Permanent repairs can sometimes proceed too, but document conditions thoroughly first so the appraisal panel can see what was there.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Can appraisal help if my insurer denied the whole claim?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Generally no. Appraisal resolves the amount of a covered loss, not whether coverage applies. For a full denial, talk to a Michigan attorney.</p></div></details></div>
